<%# One film, everywhere: poster, title, year, director, Letterboxd rating.
    Every reference to a film carries all of those — that's the house style, and
    it's why nothing renders a title on its own.

    The title link is `stretched`, which makes the whole row the link (see the
    rule in layout.erb). Anything interactive sitting in the same row — the drag
    handle, the rank menu — lifts itself back above it.

    Every link that leaves the site targets the same named window, so a run of
    film titles lands in one Letterboxd tab instead of a dozen, and the ballot
    you were filling in is still sitting where you left it.

    No rel="noopener", and that's deliberate rather than an oversight: noopener
    forces a *fresh* browsing context and ignores the target name, so adding it
    gives you a new tab per click and no shared tab at all. The cost is that
    letterboxd.com pages opened this way can see window.opener. They can't read
    our page — cross-origin — only navigate it, and Letterboxd is the one third
    party this whole app is built on. Put the rel back if that trade ever stops
    being worth a tidy tab bar. %>
<% big = defined?(big) && big %>
<% if film.poster_url %>
  <img class="poster<%= big ? " big" : "" %>" src="<%= film.poster_url %>" alt=""
       loading="lazy" width="<%= big ? 70 : 46 %>" height="<%= big ? 105 : 69 %>">
<% end %>
<span class="title">
  <a class="stretched" href="<%= film.letterboxd_url %>" target="letterboxd"><%= film.title %></a><% if film.year %>
    <span class="year"><%= film.year %></span><% end %>
  <br>
  <span class="meta">
    <% if film.director %>dir. <%= film.director %><% end %>
    <% if film.rating %><span class="rating">★ <%= film.rating_display %></span><% end %>
    <% if defined?(note) && note %>· <%= note %><% end %>
  </span>
</span>
